    <article>
      <section id="hardest" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">The hardest call</p>
        <h2>Every money app plans in months. Money runs in years.</h2>
        <p>The first version of this idea led with a single “safe to spend” number. It was a good number and a weak product, because every budgeting app already shows you a number for the month.</p>
        <p>The things that actually break a budget don’t run on a monthly clock. Car insurance is yearly. A car service comes round every six months or so. School fees are termly, festivals are seasonal. In a normal year there are around eight of these, and a monthly view sees none of them until they land.</p>
        <p>So I flipped the model. Twelve plans across the next twelve months, and today’s number is what falls out of that plan. The year is why the number is true. The number is why you open the app.</p>
        <blockquote>The year is the engine. Today is the surface.</blockquote>
      </section>

      <section id="who" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">Who this is for</p>
        <h2>Your balance is a lie</h2>
        <p>Picture someone on a salary in an Indian city, paying rent and an EMI, with a car and a kid in school. The bank says ₹84,000. Rent leaves on the 5th, the EMI on the 7th, and there’s a car insurance renewal on 22 March they’ve forgotten about.</p>
        <p>They fail twice. First they overspend early, because the balance looked healthy. Then in March they put ₹18,000 on a credit card. They could afford it all along. Nothing told them it was coming.</p>
        <div class="stats">
          <div><b>₹84,000</b><span>what the bank says is there</span></div>
          <div><b>8</b><span>irregular bills a year that a monthly view never sees</span></div>
          <div><b>₹18,000</b><span>the March renewal that ends up on a credit card</span></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="claim" class="lt wide">
        <div class="cl-in">
          <p class="eyebrow">Same number, different claim</p>
          <div class="vs">
            <div class="other"><span class="k">Every other app says</span><p class="say"><s>₹1,850 left this month.</s></p></div>
            <div class="tw"><span class="k">Twelve says</span><p class="say">₹1,850 safe today, because March insurance still needs <em>₹4,200 reserved</em>.</p></div>
          </div>
          <p>Same number. The second sentence is the product, and no monthly app can write it, because it needs to know what March looks like before March arrives.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="model">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why a model, not rules</p>
        <h2>A rule can’t find a bill it has seen once</h2>
        <div class="list">
          <div><span class="n">01</span><h3>Detection</h3><p>Finding a charge that happened once, eleven months ago, under a merchant name you don’t recognise, and knowing it will come back.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">02</span><h3>Prediction</h3><p>The date drifts and the amount creeps up at renewal. Both need an estimate with honest confidence attached, not a fixed rule.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">03</span><h3>Re planning</h3><p>Every new transaction re runs the whole year, so today’s number moves on its own, without anyone asking.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="flow" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">The flow</p>
          <h2>Six ways into the loop, and only two start with you</h2>
          <p>Setup happens once. Twelve reads 14 months of accounts, finds the irregular bills, and hands them back for you to correct. After that the loop runs every day. Twelve computes, notices, asks and audits itself on its own. You come in through only two doors: asking it something, or asking why.</p>
          <p>Every path ends in the same place. The twelve month plan gets re solved and tomorrow’s number changes.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="board flowfig">
          <a href="img/11-user-flow.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the user flow full size"><img class="shot wide" src="img/11-user-flow.webp" width="3520" height="2316" alt="Twelve user flow: six ways into the daily loop" loading="lazy"></a><button type="button" class="flowfs" aria-label="Open the user flow full screen"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M15 3h6v6M9 21H3v-6M21 3l-7 7M3 21l7-7"/></svg>Full screen</button>
          <figcaption>Dark boxes are Twelve acting. Light boxes are you or the screen. The app uses the same colour rule, so the flow and the UI teach each other.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="ia" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">Information architecture</p>
          <h2>No navigation, because there is one destination</h2>
          <p>Am I okay, and if not, what do I do about it? Home answers that in two states, on track and off pace. Things you ask for open from inside home. Things Twelve decides you need arrive on top. Nothing sits more than one level deep.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="board flowfig">
          <a href="img/12-information-architecture.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the information architecture full size"><img class="shot wide" src="img/12-information-architecture.webp" width="4280" height="1800" alt="Twelve information architecture: one surface, two states" loading="lazy"></a><button type="button" class="flowfs" aria-label="Open the information architecture full screen"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M15 3h6v6M9 21H3v-6M21 3l-7 7M3 21l7-7"/></svg>Full screen</button>
        </figure>
        <div class="notbuilt">
          <span class="k pr">Deliberately not built</span>
          <ul>
            <li>No transaction list.</li>
            <li>No category chart.</li>
            <li>No budgets you set yourself.</li>
            <li>No chat.</li>
            <li>No day, week or month toggle, because a switcher means the app has stopped having an opinion.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="screens" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">The screens</p>
          <h2>Nine screens, each one a different way the AI shows up</h2>
          <p>I ordered these by what the model is doing, not by where they sit in the app. Read the labels alone and you get the whole AI story.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="screens">
          <div class="screen">
            <div><span class="k">01 · AI proposes</span><h3>Here is what I found</h3><p>Setup ends with Twelve handing back what it found in 14 months of accounts: car insurance every March, school fees every April, Diwali every October, a car service it has only seen once. Each one carries a confidence label and an Edit button. The card at the top says it plainly: these are my guesses, not your data.</p></div>
            <div class="iph pm"><div class="bz"><div class="sc pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img src="img/01-rhythm-check.webp" width="780" height="2776" alt="Twelve screen: Here is what I found" loading="lazy"></div></div><i class="b1"></i><i class="b2"></i><i class="b3"></i><i class="b4"></i></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <div><span class="k">02 · AI computes</span><h3>One number, with its reason attached</h3><p>₹1,850 safe today, and right under it the reason: March insurance still needs ₹4,200. The year ahead sits below as a strip of twelve months, with the next bill and its confidence. Home changes every day without you touching it, because every spend re plans the year.</p></div>
            <div class="iph pm"><div class="bz"><div class="sc pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img src="img/02-home-on-track.webp" width="780" height="2776" alt="Twelve screen: One number, with its reason attached" loading="lazy"></div></div><i class="b1"></i><i class="b2"></i><i class="b3"></i><i class="b4"></i></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <div><span class="k">03 · AI shows its working</span><h3>Tap the number and it opens its working</h3><p>Every input it used, and how sure it is about each one. It points out its own weakest guess (the car service, seen once) and lets you switch it off to see the number move from ₹1,850 to ₹1,940. This is the screen that proves it’s a model and not a formula.</p></div>
            <div class="iph pm"><div class="bz"><div class="sc pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img src="img/03-why-1850.webp" width="780" height="2648" alt="Twelve screen: Tap the number and it opens its working" loading="lazy"></div></div><i class="b1"></i><i class="b2"></i><i class="b3"></i><i class="b4"></i></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <div><span class="k">04 · You ask</span><h3>It only offers questions it can answer</h3><p>There’s no blank chat box. Ask Twelve opens a short list of questions the model has enough data to answer well, grouped by today, the year and the plan. At the bottom it says what it won’t do: guess.</p></div>
            <div class="iph pm"><div class="bz"><div class="sc pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img src="img/04-ask-twelve.webp" width="780" height="2358" alt="Twelve screen: It only offers questions it can answer" loading="lazy"></div></div><i class="b1"></i><i class="b2"></i><i class="b3"></i><i class="b4"></i></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <div><span class="k">05 · AI answers live</span><h3>Type a number, watch tomorrow move</h3><p>A number pad, not a text field. Type ₹14,000 and before you spend a rupee it tells you what happens: past ₹4,200 this starts eating March, and tomorrow’s number drops from ₹1,850 to ₹1,240. Adjust the plan, or leave it. Nothing changes until you say so.</p></div>
            <div class="iph pm"><div class="bz"><div class="sc pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img src="img/05-can-i-afford-this.webp" width="780" height="2012" alt="Twelve screen: Type a number, watch tomorrow move" loading="lazy"></div></div><i class="b1"></i><i class="b2"></i><i class="b3"></i><i class="b4"></i></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <div><span class="k">06 · AI admits it doesn’t know</span><h3>₹8,400 went to Sharma Motors and I’ve never seen it before</h3><p>When something new lands, Twelve refuses to guess. It asks one question (every year, every few months, or one off), shows exactly what your answer will change, and promises to ask only once.</p></div>
            <div class="iph pm"><div class="bz"><div class="sc pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img src="img/06-it-asks-you.webp" width="780" height="2598" alt="Twelve screen: ₹8,400 went to Sharma Motors and I’ve never seen it before" loading="lazy"></div></div><i class="b1"></i><i class="b2"></i><i class="b3"></i><i class="b4"></i></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <div><span class="k">07 · AI notices</span><h3>You just spent ₹14,000</h3><p>This arrives after the spend, on purpose. A UPI payment can’t be stopped mid flight in India, so a screen that pretends to catch you before you pay would be lying. It states the damage in terms of your year (March insurance is now ₹6,400 short) and gives you two real paths. It never blocks anything.</p></div>
            <div class="iph pm"><div class="bz"><div class="sc pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img src="img/07-intervention.webp" width="780" height="2190" alt="Twelve screen: You just spent ₹14,000" loading="lazy"></div></div><i class="b1"></i><i class="b2"></i><i class="b3"></i><i class="b4"></i></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <div><span class="k">08 · AI holds the line</span><h3>Off pace, and the way back</h3><p>The same home, second state. ₹740 safe today, down from ₹1,850, with what drifted (food delivery is ₹2,900 over across 11 orders) and two ways back, each with its cost: hold ₹740 a day for 19 days, or spend ₹950 and push part of the March reserve.</p></div>
            <div class="iph pm"><div class="bz"><div class="sc pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img src="img/08-home-off-pace.webp" width="780" height="2614" alt="Twelve screen: Off pace, and the way back" loading="lazy"></div></div><i class="b1"></i><i class="b2"></i><i class="b3"></i><i class="b4"></i></div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <div><span class="k">09 · AI audits itself</span><h3>I got two things wrong</h3><p>At month end Twelve marks its own homework. Car service came in 40% over its estimate, electricity 62% over. It says so, raises the next car service estimate to ₹8,900, and tells you March is still covered.</p></div>
            <div class="iph pm"><div class="bz"><div class="sc pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img src="img/09-month-close.webp" width="780" height="3026" alt="Twelve screen: I got two things wrong" loading="lazy"></div></div><i class="b1"></i><i class="b2"></i><i class="b3"></i><i class="b4"></i></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="wrong" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">What I got wrong first</p>
        <h2>The screens were rebuilt four times</h2>
        <div class="fixes">
          <div class="fix"><div><span class="k pr">Mistake</span><h3>The intervention assumed I could catch a payment before it happened.</h3></div><div><span class="k">Fix</span><p>Nothing in India can intercept a UPI or card payment in the moment. I reframed it as “You just spent”, a notice after the fact that openly says nothing has changed yet, and added a way in before spending (Ask Twelve) so the “before” moment still exists, started by the person.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fix"><div><span class="k pr">Mistake</span><h3>The screens said they were AI without showing it.</h3></div><div><span class="k">Fix</span><p>First person copy and a signature badge still read as a forecasting app. What actually reads as AI is visible reasoning, live answers, named uncertainty and the model changing in front of you. That’s why “Why ₹1,850”, “It asks you” and the live number pad exist.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fix"><div><span class="k pr">Mistake</span><h3>Home led with the year.</h3></div><div><span class="k">Fix</span><p>An annual planner gives nobody a reason to open it daily. Today’s number moved to the top with the year as its stated reason, and the twelve month strip dropped to second.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fix"><div><span class="k pr">Mistake</span><h3>The colour was cool on cool.</h3></div><div><span class="k">Fix</span><p>A mint accent on a green ground had no temperature contrast. I moved to forest and cream with a single citrus accent, so the moments when Twelve speaks (cream panels) break the dark instead of blending into it.</p></div></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="tradeoffs" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">Trade offs</p>
        <h2>Every decision closed a door</h2>
        <div class="trades">
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">01</span><div><h3>Annual engine, daily surface</h3><p>The year is why the number is true. The number is why you open the app.</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">What it cost</span>Someone skimming home may take it for a budgeting app before they read the line underneath.</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">02</span><div><h3>One question, not a dashboard</h3><p>Anything that doesn’t answer “am I okay” was cut.</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">What it cost</span>No transaction list, no category breakdown. People who like browsing their spending aren’t served at all.</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">03</span><div><h3>An opinion, never a veto</h3><p>The intervention states the consequence and offers two paths. It never blocks a payment.</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">What it cost</span>Some people will spend anyway and end the month short.</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">04</span><div><h3>Correction by touch, not by chat</h3><p>Every forecast is tappable and editable.</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">What it cost</span>A correction a sentence could express easily now needs direct manipulation.</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">05</span><div><h3>Confidence over precision</h3><p>“Around ₹18,000, medium confidence” is trusted more than a false ₹18,412.</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">What it cost</span>The interface keeps reminding you it might be wrong.</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">06</span><div><h3>Two states, one surface</h3><p>On track and off pace share home, because they’re the same question with different answers.</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">What it cost</span>The off pace state can’t have a layout of its own.</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">07</span><div><h3>One way in, and it only offers what it can answer</h3><p>A set of answerable questions instead of a blank field.</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">What it cost</span>A question outside the set has no route.</p></div>
          <div class="trade"><span class="n">08</span><div><h3>No navigation, because there is one destination</h3><p>Sheets open from home, takeovers arrive on top.</p></div><p class="cost"><span class="k">What it cost</span>Settings, history and deeper reports have nowhere to live yet.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="identity" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">Identity</p>
          <h2>Light marks who is speaking</h2>
          <p>The app sits in deep forest. When Twelve speaks, the surface turns cream, so the system is the thing that breaks the dark. The mark is a year that doesn’t quite close, with the next thing you need to be ready for sitting in the gap. It signs every panel the system speaks through, so it has to survive at 22px.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="board">
          <a href="img/13-identity.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the identity board full size"><img class="shot wide" src="img/13-identity.webp" width="4280" height="2600" alt="Twelve identity: mark, colour and type" loading="lazy"></a>
        </figure>
        <div class="rules">
          <div><h3>Numbers are never abbreviated</h3><p>₹18,000 stays ₹18,000, never 18K.</p></div>
          <div><h3>No shadows, no gradients.</h3></div>
          <div><h3>Confidence is written, not implied.</h3></div>
          <div><h3>Cream appears once per screen.</h3></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="changed" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">What changed</p>
        <h2>The same ₹18,000 bill, two different Marches</h2>
        <div class="beforeafter">
          <div class="b"><span class="k">Before (a monthly app)</span><p>The balance looks healthy all year. March arrives, the ₹18,000 renewal lands, and it goes on a credit card.</p></div>
          <div class="a"><span class="k">After (Twelve)</span><p>The renewal is found in setup, seen twice, high confidence. Twelve sets money aside for it every month, so by August ₹13,800 is already held and only ₹4,200 is left to find. That ₹4,200 is the reason today’s number is ₹1,850, and in March the money is simply there.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p>The number I’d measure is the share of irregular bills that arrive already fully reserved. A monthly app scores close to zero here by design, because it never sees those bills coming. In the worked example, that’s the gap between a ₹18,000 renewal landing on a credit card and landing on money that’s already set aside. If Twelve works, this share climbs every month, and the month close screen is where you’d watch it happen.</p>
        <div class="metric"><span class="k">The one metric that matters</span><b>Bills that arrive already covered</b></div>
      </section>

      <section id="breaks" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow pr">Stated plainly</p>
        <h2>Where this breaks</h2>
        <p>Year one is weaker than year two. Until it has your history, Twelve guesses from category priors and asks questions, and a bill it learns about in July has fewer months left to reserve for, so the daily number drops.</p>
        <p>Forecast accuracy for irregular bills needs checking against real statement data before this ships.</p>
        <p>And only one of the three rhythms (daily glance, the nudge after a spend, the month close) depends on you remembering to open the app. That’s honest, and it’s also the part I’m least sure about.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="learned">
        <p class="eyebrow">What I learned</p>
        <h2>You can’t assert AI. You have to show it working.</h2>
        <p>My early screens said “I” everywhere and carried a little Twelve badge, and they still read as a forecasting app. Adding more AI language never fixed that. What fixed it was letting the model do visible things: open its working, answer as you type, admit it doesn’t know, and mark its own homework at month end.</p>
        <p>The other lesson came from the payment rails. I designed a beautiful “about to spend” moment before checking whether anything in India could actually trigger it. Nothing can. Now I check the real constraint before I design the screen that depends on it.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>

